Context: A system must accept commands during a temporary database outage. Commands are financially consequential and must not be lost. The business forbids confirming acceptance unless the command is durably recorded.
Return success immediately and store commands in process memory,Retry the database call asynchronously after returning success,Write to a durable acceptance log or queue before acknowledgment and apply commands idempotently when the database recovers,Reject every command for the entire outage even when a separate durable acceptance mechanism is available

Context: An autoscaler observes low average CPU and scales down during a latency incident. One partition is overloaded, while many workers are idle because traffic distribution is skewed. The router cannot change partition ownership instantly.
Lower the CPU target so all workers remain permanently provisioned,Increase total worker count without changing routing,Scale based only on average CPU because latency is noisy,Scale using per-partition backlog and service-time signals while addressing skew and bounding rebalancing disruption

Context: A database unique constraint prevents duplicate account creation. Two requests race: one transaction inserts the account and commits, while the other receives a uniqueness violation. The second request may be a legitimate retry of the same logical command.
Remove the unique constraint and check for duplicates in application code,Treat every uniqueness violation as a successful retry,Return an error without inspecting the existing record,Use a stable command identity and verify that the conflicting record represents the same intended operation before returning its result

Context: A service stores timestamps from clients in multiple time zones. A policy allows one action per rolling 24-hour interval, and clients can submit timestamps that differ from server time. Clock skew is unbounded within the client contract.
Normalize client timestamps to UTC and trust them,Use calendar-day buckets based on the user's time zone,Enforce the interval using authoritative server-side time and atomic state transitions rather than client-supplied timestamps,Reject all requests whose client timestamp is not exactly equal to server time

Context: A streaming job computes five-minute windows. Events can arrive twenty minutes late, and results are published to a downstream system that cannot retract already-issued alerts. The business wants late corrections but forbids contradictory duplicate alerts.
Set the watermark to five minutes and discard all late events,Keep every window open forever,Publish every recalculated result as a new independent alert,Separate provisional from final results and define correction-aware downstream identity and semantics before publishing revisions

Context: A deployment changes the meaning of a persisted enum value without changing its numeric representation. Old binaries continue running and interpret the same value differently. The database schema itself remains valid.
Rename the enum member and deploy normally,Add a database index on the enum column,Use versioned representations or a compatibility migration that prevents mixed-version semantic ambiguity,Keep the same numeric values and rely on backward compatibility

Context: A distributed database uses five replicas and requires three acknowledgments for writes. During a partition, two replicas are reachable from one side and three from the other. Reads may be stale, but conflicting committed values must not both be accepted as authoritative.
Allow both partitions to accept writes and resolve by timestamp,Lower the write quorum to two so both sides remain available,Use a single replica as leader without fencing old leaders,Preserve intersecting quorum or lease/fencing guarantees and reject writes on the side that cannot establish authority

Context: A batch processor marks a batch complete before all external side effects finish. A crash after the completion marker causes recovery to skip unfinished effects. Rerunning the entire batch would duplicate already completed effects.
Move the completion marker earlier to reduce recovery time,Use per-item durable completion state and idempotent or reconcilable side effects,Increase batch size to reduce marker writes,Disable recovery and manually rerun failed batches

Context: A service accepts compressed uploads. A small compressed request can expand to enormous data and consume all memory before validation finishes. Legitimate clients need large uploads, and request-size limits alone are insufficient.
Raise the compressed request-size limit,Trust the declared uncompressed length in request metadata,Stream decompression with enforced expanded-size ratio nesting and resource limits,Decompress entirely in memory and reject only after allocation completes

Context: A leader election system grants leadership based on heartbeats. A network pause prevents the old leader from receiving renewal responses, while its downstream database remains reachable. The new leader also has database access.
Reduce the heartbeat interval so elections happen faster,Let both leaders continue and rely on application-level retries,Make the database reject writes from stale leadership epochs or fencing tokens,Increase the election timeout until network pauses are rare

Context: A shared database connection pool serves multiple tenants. One tenant issues long-running queries and occupies most connections. Other tenants have short queries and strict latency objectives. The database still has spare CPU capacity.
Increase the global pool size until the queue disappears,Cancel every query exceeding the fastest tenant's deadline,Move every tenant to a separate database immediately,Apply per-tenant concurrency and workload isolation with bounded queues and query deadlines

Context: A replicated object catalog stores metadata separately from object bytes. Uploading bytes succeeds, but the metadata commit fails. A retry may upload the same object again, and orphaned bytes must eventually be reclaimed without deleting objects that became referenced later.
Delete every object whose upload timestamp is old,Retry metadata writes without tracking object identity,Use deterministic object identity and a reconciliation process that checks authoritative references before deleting orphan candidates,Commit metadata first and assume object upload cannot fail

Context: A service must migrate identifiers from integers to globally unique strings. Some downstream consumers compare identifiers numerically, old events remain in retention, and rollback to the old application version may be required.
Change the column type and deploy all services simultaneously,Generate string IDs and let numeric consumers coerce them,Keep integer IDs as the permanent public identifier,Use a staged dual-representation migration with explicit event/schema versioning backfill compatibility checks and a rollback boundary

Context: A recommendation service has good overall model quality, but errors are concentrated in a small tenant with unusual data. Global accuracy remains stable after a new model release, while the affected tenant's critical decisions deteriorate.
Roll back only if global accuracy drops below the release threshold,Increase the model's global confidence threshold,Aggregate more traffic before investigating,Use tenant- and slice-level evaluation with a guarded rollout and a rollback trigger tied to the affected decision risk

Context: A job orchestrator records a step as successful after receiving a response from a worker. The worker may have completed the action but the response can be lost. Re-executing the step is unsafe, and the worker's state is queryable by a stable operation ID.
Assume a lost response means the worker failed,Retry with a new operation ID,Disable worker timeouts so responses are never lost,Query the authoritative operation state by stable ID and resume from the confirmed outcome

Context: A database is approaching storage exhaustion because old versions of frequently updated rows remain in the write-ahead log archive. Replication is healthy, and backups are recent, but point-in-time recovery is required for a defined retention period.
Delete archived logs older than the latest backup,Increase database storage and ignore retention policy,Disable point-in-time recovery to reduce archive growth,Set archive retention from the recovery objective and verified backup chain then reclaim only logs no longer needed by any supported restore path

Context: An eventually consistent shopping cart receives offline changes from two devices. Both add different items and later synchronize. Quantity changes for the same item must preserve both independent increments, while an explicit user removal must not be undone by delayed replay.
Resolve each item by choosing the latest device timestamp,Merge cart snapshots by selecting the one with more items,Use operation-aware merge semantics that preserve increments and represent removals with causal or versioned state,Reject offline updates whenever two devices changed the cart

Context: A security-sensitive endpoint validates a token, then performs a privileged action several seconds later. The token can be revoked during that interval, and revocation must take effect before the action begins, not merely before the request arrives.
Check token expiry only at request entry,Use shorter-lived tokens without checking current revocation state,Revalidate or enforce a current authorization epoch at the action boundary with a defined consistency guarantee,Cache token validation for the entire request to avoid repeated checks

Context: A distributed database performs online schema evolution. A new index build is incomplete when the new application version begins relying on it for latency-sensitive queries. Queries remain correct but begin exceeding their deadlines.
Force all traffic onto the new version to finish the rollout faster,Increase query timeouts until index construction completes,Disable the new index and keep all new query patterns active,Gate traffic or query-plan reliance on verified index readiness and provide a safe fallback until the build is complete

Context: A multi-tenant event processor must preserve strict order for each invoice but can process different invoices concurrently. A retry for one invoice must not block all other invoices, and a poison event must not allow later events for that invoice to overtake it.
Use one global FIFO queue and stop on any failed event,Distribute events randomly across consumers and rely on retries,Send every failed event directly to a dead-letter queue and continue the invoice,Use invoice-keyed ordered lanes with isolated retries and an explicit per-invoice poison-event policy

Context: A deployment introduces a new mandatory API response field. Some consumers reject unknown fields, some require the new field, and all consumers cannot be upgraded atomically. The field is necessary for a new workflow but not for old workflows.
Add the field to every response immediately,Send the field only when a request asks for the new workflow,Remove all old response fields to force consumer upgrades,Return the field randomly during rollout to measure compatibility

Context: A service's latency improves after a cache change, but stale-data incidents increase. Cache invalidation is best-effort, and a record can change from active to legally blocked. Serving a blocked record is a critical violation.
Set the cache TTL to a very small value and keep best-effort invalidation,Add more invalidation retries but do not change read behavior,Cache only records that have never changed,Keep ordinary data cacheable but enforce the legally blocked state through an authoritative check or version barrier that stale cache entries cannot bypass

Context: A replicated state machine replays commands after leader recovery. Commands contain a relative instruction such as "expire this reservation in 30 seconds." Replaying the command later would compute a different expiration time.
Store only the relative duration and recompute it during replay,Skip all commands older than the current leader's startup time,Use the receiving node's local wall clock for every replay,Persist the resolved absolute deadline or deterministic logical-time input as part of the original committed command

Context: A batch import partially updates 80,000 records before failing. The source file is immutable, some records are valid independently, and operators need to resume without reprocessing successful records or leaving the dataset in an ambiguous state.
Wrap the entire import in one transaction regardless of duration,Restart the import from the beginning and overwrite existing rows,Skip every record whose timestamp predates the retry,Use deterministic record identity per-record outcomes and durable checkpoints with idempotent writes and explicit batch completion state

Context: A global API router chooses the nearest region. A region is reachable and passes shallow health checks but has a broken dependency used only by one critical endpoint. Routing every request away from that region would overload the remaining regions.
Route all traffic to the nearest reachable region,Fail over all traffic whenever any dependency check fails,Increase timeouts for the critical endpoint,Use dependency- and capability-aware routing with endpoint-specific failover capacity limits and a degraded-mode policy